<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-fractions">
  <title>Fractions and Rational Numbers</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-number-fractions"><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol>
      <li><p>Explain a fraction as equal parts, a quotient and a rational number.</p></li>
      <li><p>Form and compare equivalent fractions.</p></li>
      <li><p>Perform arithmetic with fractions without dividing by zero.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
  <introduction><p>A fraction is one number obtained from a quotient or equal parts of a whole. We investigate how its representation can change without changing its value.</p></introduction>
  <section xml:id="fractions-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>A fraction describes part of a whole, a quotient, or a multiplicative comparison. Equivalent fractions have the same value even when their numerators and denominators differ.</p></introduction>
    <p>Every rational number is <m>\frac{a}{b}</m> for integers <m>a,b</m>, where <m>b\ne0</m>. Dividing a whole into equal parts explains why <m>\frac{2}{3}=\frac{4}{6}</m>.</p>
    <p>To add fractions, use a common denominator. To multiply fractions, multiply numerators and denominators, then simplify where possible.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="fractions-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-fractions">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Calculate 1/3+1/6.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Use sixths: 2/6+1/6=3/6=1/2.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-fractions">
      <statement><p>Calculate 3/4 of 20.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>3/4×20=15.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
